STRIDED-RECTANGLE BORROW RECORDS: sharding a picture plane by COLUMN (issue #455)
================================================================================

Host      Apple M4 Pro, 8 P + 4 E cores (12 logical), Darwin 25.5.2. Idle box.
Base      main ee07b00 (the #467 composition; 766/766).
Branch    perf/strided-rect.
Build     cargo build --release, default features, NO -C target-cpu=native.
Timing    NO `nice` on any timed run (Darwin maps a niced process onto E-cores
          and the wall clock distorts ~40x). Two-point wall fit at 2 and 20
          frames, `beta = (hi - lo) / 18`, so process startup drops out.
          dav1d 1.5.4 `--framedelay 1` measured in the SAME interleaved sweep,
          rotating arm order inside every cell.
Vectors   ~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/v4k_8tile{,_10b}.avif for our arms;
          ~/tmp/recon-yard/vec/*.ivf for dav1d. `test-vectors/bench/photo_4k.avif`
          is NOT a substitute — its CDEF is off and its filter chain is a
          different shape (the diag round measured 23.7 ms against 50.5).

Files     strided_rect_gap_2026-08-09.tsv     216 raw gap rows (3 arms x 8 cells x 9)
          strided_rect_sites_2026-08-09.tsv   per-call-site registration counts
          strided_rect_bands_2026-08-09.txt   per-cell min/median/max + disjoint verdict


0. THE PROBLEM, IN ONE SENTENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A `w x h` block of a picture plane was either `h` borrow registrations of `w`
elements each, or ONE registration of the hull `(h-1)*stride + w`. Neither is
right under tile threading: the per-row form is what makes a 4K frame register
7,924,706 borrows at t=1 but 22,700,725 at t>1, and the hull form additionally
reserves the inter-row gaps, which belong to other tile COLUMNS.

The flat block index `offset >> shift` cannot break that tie, because it has to
choose between two properties at once:

  * SMALL blocks separate concurrent tile columns (two workers 960 bytes apart
    on the same picture row land on different shards) but make a strided access
    pay one distinct shard cache line PER ROW;
  * LARGE blocks keep a strided access on one line but put all four tile
    columns of a row on it.

`benchmarks/tracker_blockshift_2026-08-08.meta` is the record of that trade
being made in both directions: shift 12 measured 119.0 ms/frame at t=8, shift
14 measured 72.8, shift 16 measured 75.5, and a later attempt to go finer to
separate tile columns collapsed scaling to 2.7x.


1. THE MECHANISM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A COLUMN class has both properties at once. Every row of a `w x h` rectangle
occupies the SAME columns, so the rectangle is one class however tall it is;
two tile columns are different classes however close their rows are.

  RowMap (crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/src/tracker_shard.rs)
      offset -> (column band, row group), where the row index comes from ONE
      exact division by the declared row stride. Lemire's magic
      (`floor(2^64/d)+1`, exact for every dividend below 2^32) so it is a
      `umulh` plus an `msub`, not a ~20-cycle `udiv`. Consulted only when the
      instance is BOTH strided AND sharded, so a serial decode — which takes
      `add`'s `mask == 0` arm and skips all class arithmetic — pays nothing.

  ShardRecs.c0/.c1
      each record's inclusive column range. Hull-overlap AND column-overlap
      together are EXACT, not merely conservative: if the row ranges are
      disjoint, say ra1 < rb0, hull overlap needs (rb0-ra1)*S <= ca1-cb0, whose
      left side is at least S and whose right side is at most S-1. So there is
      no false positive to trade away. Anything the map cannot describe as a
      rectangle keeps `0..=COL_ANY` and degrades to today's plain hull test,
      which can only ever be conservative.

  StridedRows (crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/src/lib.rs)
      the index type. It deliberately does NOT implement `SliceBounds` — the
      blanket `From<T: SliceBounds> for Bounds` would erase the shape back to a
      plain interval — and the shape travels as two scalar arguments to
      `add_rect_*` rather than as `Bounds` fields, because growing `Bounds`
      from 16 to 24 bytes measured **+3.6% at 8bpc t=1** on its own.

Converted callers: `for_rows{,_mut}`, `narrow_guard{,_mut}`,
`compact_read_per_row`, `compact_write_back_per_row`, `block_mut`, and the loop
filter's `LfBlock::fill`. `block_mut` in particular stops COPYING under tile
threading: it used to read `h` rows into a scratch buffer and write them back,
`2h` registrations plus two memcpys per block, purely because per-row guards
were the only sound option.

SLOTS drops 7 -> 5 so the record still fits one 128-byte cache line. Measured
mean shard occupancy is 0.02 and measured max is 1, and slot exhaustion is not
a correctness event — it promotes to the wide list. Isolated A/B of the SLOTS
change alone on ee07b00: 6750 vs base 6760 ms for 20 frames at 8bpc t=1
(median of 3) — null.


2. COUNTS (`--features probe-sites`, v4k_8tile 8bpc, per frame)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                          base            head
    t=1               7,924,706       7,924,706
    t=8              22,700,725       7,923,518

**The thread-count-dependent explosion is gone.** t=8 is now within 1,188
registrations of t=1 — the residual is `picwb`-class traffic, not per-row
guards. The base column reproduces #467's independently-counted totals exactly.


3. TWO DEFECTS FOUND BY MEASUREMENT, BOTH NOW GATED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(a) THE WIDE-PATH AVALANCHE. `add_grid` escalated to the all-shards path
    whenever `state != 0` — i.e. whenever any wide record was live. One wide
    record then makes every straddling borrow wide, each of which keeps `state`
    nonzero, so the population never drains. Measured with `--features
    probe-wide` on v4k_8tile:

        wide registrations/frame     t=1      t=2      t=8
        with the escalation            0    2,309  188,307
        poison-only                    0    2,220    2,220

    (t=4 not measured for this counter. `Located::All` is pure geometry, so the
    poison-only row being IDENTICAL at t=2 and t=8 is the check that the
    feedback loop is gone — with the escalation it was 81x higher at t=8.)

    and wall at t=8 was **2.28x WORSE than base** before the fix. Only poison
    escalates now; `lock_and_register` consults the wide list under the locks it
    takes, exactly as `add_multi` already did.

(b) `rect_exact_for` MUST REQUIRE `mask != 0`. `add` dispatches `mask == 0`
    first — a one-shard instance skips all class arithmetic because every class
    maps to shard 0 anyway — so on such an instance a rectangle is recorded as
    its plain HULL however well the map describes it. Answering `true` there
    tells a caller it may stop taking per-row borrows while the tracker is still
    reserving the gaps: exactly the false positive this change exists to remove.
    Caught by the EXISTING gate
    `picture.rs::for_rows_mut_never_reserves_an_inter_row_gap_when_tile_threading_is_on`,
    whose 64x4 plane is under `SHARD_MIN_LEN` and therefore always single-shard.


4. THE COLUMN-BAND WIDTH IS A MEASURED CHOICE, AND BOTH ENDS LOSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`col_shift = ilog2(stride) - COL_BAND_ADJ`. v4k_8tile 8bpc, median of 3, idle,
wall ms for 20 frames against base 2290 (t=4) / 1400 (t=8). All rows have (a)
fixed and NONE of them has the loop filter's `LfBlock::fill` converted yet — the
sweep picked the band width, and the loop filter (3,835,042 registrations per
frame at t=8, the largest single site) landed after it. So these absolutes are
not the shipped ones; the RATIOS between rows are what chose the constant.

Rows 3-7 are one binary selected by an env knob, which was deleted afterwards;
row 1 is a separate compile-time build.

      ADJ  bands/row   t=4 ms  ratio    t=8 ms  ratio
        1      4         2080  0.912      2240  1.600
        3      8         1990  0.869      1540  1.100
        4     15         2000  0.873      1460  1.043
        5     30         2010  0.878      1390  0.993   <- COL_BAND_ADJ
        6     60         2060  0.900      1420  1.014
        7    120         2160  0.943      1490  1.064

Too few bands and concurrent tile columns share a shard (ADJ 1 is 1.600x at
t=8 with ZERO wide promotions — the wide path was not the problem there,
collision was). Too many and a borrow straddles several and takes the
multi-shard path.


