  v4k_8tile_10b    8   71.2 [ 67.9.. 73.4]   60.4 [ 57.8.. 63.3]   37.2 [ 35.6.. 38.3]

  vector           t  head/base   bands     base/ref  head/ref
  -------------------------------------------------------------
  v4k_8tile        1     1.0143   OVERLAP      1.291     1.309
  v4k_8tile        2     0.8433  disjoint      1.580     1.332
  v4k_8tile        4     0.8115  disjoint      1.631     1.323
  v4k_8tile        8     0.7848  disjoint      1.882     1.477
  v4k_8tile_10b    1     1.0141   OVERLAP      1.426     1.446
  v4k_8tile_10b    2     0.9148  disjoint      1.616     1.478
  v4k_8tile_10b    4     0.8919  disjoint      1.655     1.476
  v4k_8tile_10b    8     0.8487  disjoint      1.916     1.626

SCALING t1 -> t8:  8bpc base 4.74x -> head 6.13x (dav1d 6.91x)
                  10bpc base 5.03x -> head 6.01x (dav1d 6.76x)

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, FIRST. **t=1 is 1.4% SLOWER and the bands OVERLAP**, so
the delta is not resolvable at n=9 — but the median is enough to move 8bpc t=1
from 1.291 (the campaign's one cell UNDER the 1.30x bar) to 1.309, just over it.
That is a real cost of this change and it is not hidden by the t>1 wins. The
residual is the two column stores every `alloc` now makes and the `index_rect`
plumbing: an UNSOUND measurement arm with the stores deleted read 0.991 of head
at 8bpc t=1 (median of 3), which would put the cell at ~1.297. Making that arm
sound needs a `COLS` const parameter on `alloc`/`find`, selected by the
`mask == 0` dispatch (a one-shard instance can never carry real columns, since
only the row-map arm writes them and that arm needs `mask != 0`), plus a reset
of the column fields in `reprovision`, because `mask` can in principle fall back
to 0 when a container shrinks below `SHARD_MIN_LEN` and stale narrow columns
would then be a MISSED overlap rather than a false positive. Not attempted here.

AND THE BAR IS STILL MET NOWHERE. 1.309 / 1.332 / 1.323 / 1.477 at 8bpc.

What it does do: at t=2 and t=4 the remaining gap is within 0.03-0.04 of the
UNTRACKED CEILING the diagnosis round measured (1.307 / 1.281 with the tracker
compiled out), i.e. essentially all of the tracker's t>1 cost is now gone. The
scaling deficit that motivated the whole round — 4.74x against dav1d's 6.91x —
is 6.13x against 6.91x, and #455's own arithmetic target (hold t=1, apply
dav1d's ratios: t=2 -> 163, t=4 -> 85.6, t=8 -> 46.7) is met to within 2% at
t=2 and t=4 (166.8 and 86.8) and missed by 13% at t=8 (52.7).

10bpc moves less because a larger share of its gap was never the tracker: it
starts at 1.426 at t=1, where 8bpc starts at 1.291. That per-thread throughput
difference is a separate problem and this change does not touch it.


6. NOT MEASURED — SAY IT BEFORE THE WINS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * x86_64. Nothing here was built or run on it. The row map's cost is one
    `umulh` + `msub` on aarch64; the x86 lowering of a u128 multiply-high is
    `mulx`/`mul`, and the shard-line traffic this removes is more expensive on
    a machine with a heavier coherence protocol, so the sign is likely the same
    and the magnitude is NOT predictable. Do not extrapolate.
  * Any vector with LOOP RESTORATION live. Both 4K gap vectors have LR off and
    no superres, while LR is active in 696/768 corpus vectors, so
    `looprestoration*.rs` registers zero borrows in this grid. The corpus gate
    covers correctness there; the SPEED of LR-live content is unmeasured.
  * t=16, 1-tile 4K, and every size below 4K, for the timed grid. (The corpus
    gate covers 766 vectors of every size for correctness.)
  * Negative strides. `set_row_stride` takes `|stride|` so plain intervals are
    still classified, but every rectangle caller checks `pxstride > 0` and falls
    back, so the rectangle path is UNEXERCISED on a negative-stride picture.
    Reachable only through a c-ffi `Dav1dPicAllocator`.
  * `--features unchecked` (compiles; not benchmarked) and `tracker-legacy`
    (compiles, `rect_exact_for` returns false so every caller keeps per-row).
  * Miri.
